{
  "Version": 2,
  "Name": "Lin2014-QW-DBR-N10-800",
  "Source": "A. Lin, Y.-K. Zhong, S.-M. Fu, C. W. Tseng, and S. L. Yan, \"Aperiodic and randomized dielectric mirrors: alternatives to metallic back reflectors for solar cells,\" Opt. Express 22(S3), A880-A894 (2014). doi:10.1364/OE.22.00A880 - Sec. 3, p. A885 ('If a quarter-wavelength-slab periodic DBR with N=10 and center wavelength at lambda=800 nm is used instead...'); layer sequence from Fig. 2 (N pairs, 2N layers, t1 = nH TiO2 ... t2N = nL SiO2); Table 1 p. A890",
  "Notes": "Reference quarter-wave periodic TiO2/SiO2 DBR back reflector (N = 10 pairs, centre 800 nm) that the paper compares against its aperiodic A-DBR\nSpecification: High reflectance as a solar-cell back reflector over 400-1000 nm (the paper's target band); quarter-wave stack centred at 800 nm.\nReported: No standalone R/T numbers for this exact N=10 / 800 nm stack. In the full cell (SiO2 Mie-grating ARC + c-Si absorber + this DBR) the AM1.5 integrated absorbance is 0.525 for a 300 nm Si absorber and 0.984 for a 30 um absorber (Sec. 3 p. A885; Table 1 p. A890) - not reproducible by a planar calculation. For periodic DBRs generally the paper says the reflection bandwidth is ~350 nm (Fig. 3 right, N=5 and N=20 curves, centre not stated; Sec. 3 p. A884) and ~300 nm for SiO2/TiO2 at 400-1000 nm targets (Introduction p. A882). (Extractor estimate, not from the paper: a QW TiO2/SiO2 stopband at 800 nm is ~280-300 nm wide.)\nNotes: Rebuilt from the closed-form description (quarter-wave slabs, N = 10 pairs, lambda0 = 800 nm). Layer order from Fig. 2: t1 is TiO2 (nH) next to the absorber side, alternating to t2N = SiO2; the paper's numbering starts at the side facing the light (the Si absorber), same as here. The paper uses Rsoft-database TiO2/SiO2 with no printed indices, so the generic database TiO2 and SiO2 dispersions are used and the QWOTs are evaluated at 800 nm. In the paper light reaches the mirror from the c-Si absorber (through a SiO2 grating layer) and Sec. 6 uses n = 3.1 above the mirrors; crystalline Si is not available here, so the incident medium is set to air for a standalone mirror check - with a Si incident medium the stopband is the same but the in-band R and the side lobes differ. The medium below the last SiO2 layer is not stated; SiO2 is assumed, so the last SiO2 layer has no optical effect at normal incidence; it is kept so the list has the paper's 2N = 20 layers. Paper averages s and p (polarisation angle 45 deg) at normal incidence. 20 layers, no printed total thickness to check.\nLayers from Sec. 3, p. A885 ('If a quarter-wavelength-slab periodic DBR with N=10 and center wavelength at lambda=800 nm is used instead...'); layer sequence from Fig. 2 (N pairs, 2N layers, t1 = nH TiO2 ... t2N = nL SiO2); Table 1 p. A890; layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nNot checked: the paper reports this design only as plots.",
